/**
* @brief Fuzz target for symbolic_to_physical().
*
* This function is the final stage for compressing a block of ASTC data. It
* takes a symbolic representation of the block and packs it into the 16 byte
* physical on-disk format.
*/

#include "/src/astc-encoder/Source/astcenc_internal.h"

#include <fuzzer/FuzzedDataProvider.h>
#include <array>
#include <vector>

struct BlockSizes
{
int x;
int y;
int z;
};

// A representative set of block sizes to test against
std::array<BlockSizes, 3> testSz {{
{ 4,  4, 1}, // Highest bitrate
{12, 12, 1}, // Largest 2D block
{6,  6,  6}  // Largest 3D block
}};

std::array<block_size_descriptor, 3> testBSD;

/**
* @brief Utility function to create all of the block size descriptors needed.
*
* This is triggered once via a static initializer.
*
* Triggering once is important so that we only create a single BSD per block
* size we need, rather than one per fuzzer iteration (it's expensive). This
* improves fuzzer throughput by ~ 1000x!
*
* Triggering via a static initializer, rather than a lazy init in the fuzzer
* function, is important because is means that the BSD is allocated before
* fuzzing starts. This means that leaksanitizer will ignore the fact that we
* "leak" the dynamic allocations inside the BSD (we never call term()).
*/
bool bsd_initializer()
{
for (size_t i = 0; i < testSz.size(); i++)
{
init_block_size_descriptor(
testSz[i].x,
testSz[i].y,
testSz[i].z,
false,
4,
1.0f,
testBSD[i]);
}

return true;
}

extern "C" int LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(const uint8_t *data, size_t size)
{
// Preinitialize the block size descriptors we need
static bool init = bsd_initializer();

if (size < 1)
{
return 0;
}

FuzzedDataProvider stream(data, size);

// Select a block size to test
int i = stream.ConsumeIntegralInRange<int>(0, testSz.size() - 1);
const block_size_descriptor& bsd = testBSD[i];

// Populate the symbolic block
symbolic_compressed_block scb;

// Requirement: scb.block_type must not be SYM_BTYPE_ERROR
scb.block_type = stream.ConsumeIntegralInRange<uint8_t>(SYM_BTYPE_CONST_F16, SYM_BTYPE_NONCONST);

if (scb.block_type == SYM_BTYPE_NONCONST)
{
// Requirement: scb.partition_count must be in [1, 4]
scb.partition_count = stream.ConsumeIntegralInRange<uint8_t>(1, BLOCK_MAX_PARTITIONS);
scb.color_formats_matched = stream.ConsumeBool();

// Requirement: scb.block_mode must be a valid mode for the given bsd.
if (bsd.block_mode_count_all == 0)
{
// No valid non-constant modes for this block size.
return 0;
}

uint16_t mode_idx = stream.ConsumeIntegralInRange<uint16_t>(0, bsd.block_mode_count_all - 1);
scb.block_mode = bsd.block_modes[mode_idx].mode_index;

if (scb.partition_count > 1)
{
// Requirement: scb.partition_index must be < 1024
scb.partition_index = stream.ConsumeIntegralInRange<uint16_t>(0, (1 << PARTITION_INDEX_BITS) - 1);
}
else
{
scb.partition_index = 0;
}

// Requirement: scb.color_formats[*] must be < 16
for (unsigned int p = 0; p < scb.partition_count; ++p)
{
scb.color_formats[p] = stream.ConsumeIntegralInRange<uint8_t>(0, 15);
}

// Requirement: scb.quant_mode must be in [QUANT_6, QUANT_32]
scb.quant_mode = static_cast<quant_method>(stream.ConsumeIntegralInRange<uint8_t>(QUANT_6, QUANT_32));

// Populate color values
for (unsigned int p = 0; p < scb.partition_count; ++p)
{
for (int j = 0; j < 8; ++j)
{
scb.color_values[p][j] = stream.ConsumeIntegral<uint8_t>();
}
}

// Requirement: scb.weights[*] must be in [0, 64]
for (int w = 0; w < BLOCK_MAX_WEIGHTS; ++w)
{
scb.weights[w] = stream.ConsumeIntegralInRange<uint8_t>(0, 64);
}

// plane2_component can be -1 (single plane) or 0-3 (the component for plane 2)
scb.plane2_component = stream.ConsumeIntegralInRange<int8_t>(-1, 3);
}
else // SYM_BTYPE_CONST_F16 or SYM_BTYPE_CONST_U16
{
for (int c = 0; c < BLOCK_MAX_COMPONENTS; ++c)
{
scb.constant_color[c] = stream.ConsumeIntegral<int>();
}
}


// The output buffer
uint8_t pcb[16];

// Call the function under test
symbolic_to_physical(bsd, scb, pcb);

return 0;
}
</fuzz-target>

<function-requirements>

<input>
project name: astc-encoder
function signature: void symbolic_to_physical(const struct block_size_descriptor &, const struct symbolic_compressed_block &, uint8_t *)
</input>

<description>
The symbolic_to_physical function converts a symbolic representation of a compressed ASTC block (symbolic_compressed_block) into a physical, 128-bit (16-byte) compressed block (pcb). The conversion logic depends on the block type specified in the symbolic block. For constant color blocks, it writes a constant color value. For non-constant blocks, it encodes various components like block mode, partition information, color endpoint formats, and weights into the physical block. The process involves bit manipulation and using precomputed tables. The function relies on a block_size_descriptor to get information about the block's structure, such as decimation and block modes. The callers of this function, such as compress_block, are responsible for first finding an optimal symbolic representation for a given image block, and then calling symbolic_to_physical to perform the final conversion to a physical block. Because of this, the function assumes that the input symbolic block is well-formed and encodable. An assertion at the beginning of the function checks that the block is not an error block, which would indicate a failure in the preceding compression stages.
</description>

<requirements>
<requirement>
The `block_size_descriptor` argument `bsd` must be initialized by calling `init_block_size_descriptor`. This is because the function relies on the `bsd` to provide valid block mode and decimation information.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `scb.block_type` field must be one of `SYM_BTYPE_CONST_U16`, `SYM_BTYPE_CONST_F16`, or `SYM_BTYPE_NONCONST`. `SYM_BTYPE_ERROR` will trigger an assertion failure.
</requirement>
<requirement>
When the block is non-constant, the `scb.weights` array contains unquantized weight values. These values are used as indices into tables of size 65. Therefore, the values in `scb.weights` should be in the range [0, 64].
</requirement>
<requirement>
For non-constant blocks, `scb.quant_mode` is used to index into the `color_uquant_to_scrambled_pquant_tables` array with an offset. The valid enum values for `scb.quant_mode` are from `QUANT_6` to `QUANT_32`, as smaller values would lead to a negative index.
</requirement>
<requirement>
For non-constant blocks, the `scb.color_formats` values are used to determine the number of color values to encode. An invalid format can lead to out-of-bounds access on `scb.color_values`. The upper 2 bits of the format value (`format >> 2`) must be at most 3, meaning the format value itself must be less than 16.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `pcb` output buffer must be allocated with a size of at least 16 bytes. The function writes 128 bits (16 bytes) of data into this buffer.
</requirement>
</requirements>

</function-requirements>

